        "DEFINITION": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "canonical_heading": "DEFINITION",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Idiopathic lower urinary tract disease, commonly referred to as feline idiopathic cystitis (FIC), is a nonmalignant, sterile, inflammatory disease of the urinary bladder and urethra. It is the most common urinary disorder of young to middle-aged cats and is characterized by dysuria, pollakiuria, hematuria, periuria, and in some cases urinary obstruction. Clinical observations suggest that FIC is associated with different clinical phenotypes (acute self-limiting, chronic, nonobstructive, and obstructive forms) and pathologic phenotypes (ulcerative, inflammatory, hyperplastic, nonulcerative, and noninflammatory forms). Regardless of form, the terms idiopathic lower urinary tract disease and FIC represent an exclusionary diagnosis established only after known causes have been eliminated.",
              "pdf_page": 586,
              "printed_page": 504,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "canonical_heading": "PATHOPHYSIOLOGY",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Etiopathogenesis is uncertain.",
              "pdf_page": 586,
              "printed_page": 504,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urinary bladder abnormalities include alterations in urothelial barrier structure and function, differentiation and repair, signaling, eicosanoid biosynthesis, and innate immune and inflammatory responses.",
              "pdf_page": 586,
              "printed_page": 504,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Association of FIC with stress (environ mental, psychologic, physiologic, or comorbid pathologic stressors) and identification of multiple abnormalities of nervous and endocrine systems have led to hypothesis that systemic psychoneuroendocrine factors may have a role in the pathogenesis.",
              "pdf_page": 586,
              "printed_page": 504,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Clinical and morphologic features of chronic forms of FIC are similar to those of an idiopathic cystopathy of humans called interstitial cystitis/painful bladder syndrome (IC/PBS). However, the extent to which the feline and human forms share pathogenic mechanisms has not yet been fully defined.",
              "pdf_page": 586,
              "printed_page": 504,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
        "SYSTEMS AFFECTED": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "canonical_heading": "SYSTEMS AFFECTED",
          "content_blocks": [
