    "MEDICATIONS": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "canonical_heading": "DRUG(S) OF CHOICE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Amitriptyline—empirically advocated to treat cats with severe recurrent or persistent signs; suggested dosage is 5–10 mg/cat q24h given at night; not recommended for treatment of acute, self-limiting episodes of FIC.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Butorphanol, buprenorphine, and fentanyl— have been empirically recommended for short-term analgesia in cats with FIC; there have been no reports of controlled studies to evaluate their safety or efficacy.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Prazosin—may be used to minimize reflex dyssynergia and functional urethral outflow obstruction; suggested dosage is 0.25–0.5 mg/cat PO q12–24h.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Tolteridine may be considered as an anticholinergic and antispasmodic to minimize hyperactivity of bladder detrusor muscle and urge incontinence; suggested dose is 0.05 mg/kg PO q12h; there have been no controlled studies to evaluate its safety or efficacy.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Glycosaminoglycans—empirically recommended to help repair glycosaminoglycan coating of urothelium; results of controlled clinical studies have not demonstrated any beneficial effects on reducing severity or frequency of clinical signs in cats with FIC.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Feline facial pheromone—empirically recommended to decrease signs of stress in cats with FIC; results of controlled clinical studies have not demonstrated any beneficial effects in management of FIC.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Corticosteroids—no detectable effect on remission of acute clinical signs demonstrated; predispose to bacterial UTIs, especially in cats with indwelling transurethral catheters.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs)—empirically recommended by",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "some because of their anti-inflammatory and analgesic properties; the safety of NSAIDs in the treatment of FIC has not been evaluated by controlled clinical trials.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Antibiotics—no detectable effect on remission of clinical signs in cats demonstrated.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "CONTRAINDICATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Phenazopyridine—may result in methemo globinemia and irreversible oxidative changes in hemoglobin resulting in formation of Heinz bodies and anemia.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Methylene blue—may cause Heinz bodies and severe anemia.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Bethanechol—do not use in patients with UO.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "PRECAUTIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "PRECAUTIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cats with UO and postrenal azotemia are at increased risk for adverse drug events, especially with drugs and anesthetics that depend on renal elimination or metabolism.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Indwelling transurethral catheters, especially when associated with fluidinduced diuresis, predispose patients to bacterial UTIs.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "CONTRAINDICATIONS/POSSIBLE INTERACTIONS",
          "content_blocks": []
        },
        "ALTERNATIVE DRUG(S)": {
          "present": false,
          "status": "missing",
          "source_heading": null,
          "canonical_heading": "ALTERNATIVE DRUG(S)",
          "content_blocks": []
        }
      }
    },
    "FOLLOW-UP": {
      "present": true,
      "status": "content",
      "content_blocks": [],
      "subsections": {
        "PATIENT MONITORING": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "canonical_heading": "PATIENT MONITORING",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Monitor hematuria by urinalysis; cystocentesis may cause iatrogenic hematuria, so naturally voided samples are preferred.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE",
          "canonical_heading": "PREVENTION/AVOIDANCE",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Best evidence suggests that multimodal approach to managing cats with acute nonobstructive FIC is advised, including multipurpose therapeutic urinary food proven to reduce rate of recurrent episodes of FIC signs (Hill’s c/d Multicare); environmental enrichment; feeding moist food; and shortterm administration of analgesics to control signs of pain.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS",
          "canonical_heading": "POSSIBLE COMPLICATIONS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Indwelling transurethral catheters—cause trauma; predispose to ascending bacterial UTIs.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Perineal urethrostomies—predispose to bacterial UTIs and urethral strictures.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
        "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS": {
          "present": true,
          "status": "content",
          "source_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS.",
          "canonical_heading": "EXPECTED COURSE AND PROGNOSIS",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Hematuria, dysuria, and pollakiuria often self-limiting within 4–7 days in most patients. These signs often recur unpredictably.",
              "pdf_page": 587,
              "printed_page": 505,
              "column": 3
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    },
